#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511633} :activity/id #uuid "abf9c4c3-4981-52ee-b589-3b1c3030a4cf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050567 :topic/id #uuid "54747772-1cac-572e-9b8f-be3a6c8abe45" :topic/math-academy-id 2198} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fe86c01-fa33-54c0-919e-aaad045de4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511016} {:answer/id #uuid "91cc57eb-09e7-59eb-aa0f-280a8c492891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511083} {:answer/id #uuid "94960e82-187d-589c-a1c2-ed096c94c497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511087} {:answer/id #uuid "bbaa8abc-d95e-51ca-a4d1-7a1aa516b455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511168} {:answer/id #uuid "f46a71c6-0e54-5d62-9732-93eefd0150bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511168} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdfd2c93-cc42-5e18-b039-6099f904aa29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "053eddb0-5c93-560e-b92c-a89951cbd49c" :question/math-academy-id "q-42239" :question/problem "![](images/93/938f91220033a2174c011c1f8d65ff61e81e79b8af0ef718411169364c885418.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=t\\,i+\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0]-[-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0fd57101-031a-56f6-9993-cefb5de3fe06" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511648}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1997d82a-e8fd-5876-bff6-f2a494d2c16e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510841} {:answer/id #uuid "2e996338-8bd4-5f85-acba-798d27184bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510881} {:answer/id #uuid "91c650e3-c5a3-551b-acac-5cc206d90d87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511082} {:answer/id #uuid "a50bd914-811d-5009-9a3f-16c3d9e26a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511129} {:answer/id #uuid "e5105f35-40d7-5290-bc6b-8489218cf6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511263} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f782c02-0bd7-576d-a703-443eb8f872fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "097af826-94c0-5b59-ad65-7d59555aa9e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-42221" :question/problem "![](images/44/4408d1fcee1d168c6ab21ea53cf24845093bad9cc608299a36f0510c1636cc03.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t},\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "80c380fc-d04f-536e-bc60-f772fa1cc0f7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3242ed2a-ee0f-5b6e-92a0-da63b077c141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510888} {:answer/id #uuid "482ab86f-4fe2-571e-b0ca-93e2bd5d4903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510943} {:answer/id #uuid "a3031b19-d41c-5adf-a1c1-fb61f1fe36c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511124} {:answer/id #uuid "bb43d5ac-3266-5668-89fa-191462493dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511167} {:answer/id #uuid "e19c04bb-3f86-514f-ad0d-22da5d1ba873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511250}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511167} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b42e264-51ff-5675-8ac9-a399601959a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ad2198cd-84ab-52d9-a653-128de0924655" :question/math-academy-id "q-126754" :question/problem "![](images/86/86772e27f0a3226a608a1188fd8f58b9a8956f5f02fdbc5f9c2d29e2cef2a54c.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,dx & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡2t(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-18{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =6({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a3e46484-6e96-5940-b74e-e01b342a1b8e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65573630-2003-5f37-a3a0-d25c98f4401a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510996} {:answer/id #uuid "6e780ffa-2a00-5af3-b5f3-92f795812999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511012} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbf5317-be7f-5ed0-a334-2b540864d0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511112} {:answer/id #uuid "a5484851-2c51-5273-8db2-309d04de9b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511130} {:answer/id #uuid "d89e0626-3b35-5c77-81ab-f0b1350e2a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511226}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510996} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2eb735b-b5bd-5b47-b78f-f9e6cc0794cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fdacb1ae-ddf6-5106-9485-7c1e14bd18a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-42297" :question/problem "![](images/35/35b8ad6fd40daf95ba4f77288321d6a570e2df2944d056b9445e9ba4e36f97b7.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a5cc6a28-05b8-5f80-aaa2-4f93656a403b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02757ddb-6720-5404-868b-7b1166fb2c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510781} {:answer/id #uuid "083e8245-b859-5f0f-b493-6e1eb22d754b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510797} {:answer/id #uuid "1e791730-85c4-5ff7-ad54-6e399657153c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510851} {:answer/id #uuid "3c70ca77-ec27-57f8-b078-51030fa03414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510920} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d4bd2c-dd7d-5ebf-8ab9-0d013cef2b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511284}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511284} :answer-field/id #uuid "1557ad1a-a45b-592a-8609-24271802b200" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ae04e3b-017d-534f-b76c-ac600584e435" :question/math-academy-id "q-126568" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)=-y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=y$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=6{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "eae9ae58-73f8-56ab-9f1a-1264510bfad1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511647}}] :activity/title "Using Green's Theorem to Calculate Area" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510763}]}
